{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["The reason for <b>ואלה המשפטים אשר תשים לפניהם</b> (“And these are the ordinances that you shall set before them”) is that He wished to set the ordinances before them first. For just as in the Ten Commandments the first utterance dealt with knowledge of YHWH and the second with the prohibition of idolatry, He turned back and commanded Moses: <b>כה תאמר אל בני ישראל אתם ראיתם כי מן השמים דברתי עמכם</b> (“Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: You have seen that from the heavens I have spoken with you”) (Exodus 20:22) — so that you will further warn them to set their hearts upon what they saw and to be careful concerning these commandments that I have commanded them. <b>כי אתם ראיתם</b> (“for you have seen”) corresponds to the utterance <b>אנכי</b> (“I am”), and <b>ולא תעשון אתי</b> (“you shall not make with Me”) corresponds to <b>לא יהיה לך</b> (“you shall not have”), completing the subject of idolatry. <b>ואלה המשפטים</b> (“and these are the ordinances”) corresponds to <b>לא תחמד</b> (“you shall not covet”), for if a person does not know the law of the house or the field or the rest of property, he will think it is his and covet it and take it for himself. Therefore He said <b>תשים לפניהם</b> (“you shall set before them”) — straight ordinances that will guide them among themselves, so that they will not covet what is not theirs by law. And so they said in Midrash Rabbah (Exodus 30:15): the entire Torah depends on משפט (law), therefore HaKadosh Baruch Hu gave laws after the Ten Commandments. And likewise He will explain in אלה המשפטים the law concerning idolatry (Exodus 22:19), and honoring the father (Exodus 21:15, 17), and murder (Exodus 21:12, 14), and adultery (Exodus 22:18), which are mentioned in the Ten Commandments.\n\nAnd they expounded “לפניהם” (“before them”) and not before gentiles (Tanhuma 1; Gittin 88b), because it should have properly said אשר תשים להם (“that you shall set for them”), as it says (Exodus 15:25) “שם שם לו חק ומשפט” (“there He set for him a statute and an ordinance”), yet He said “לפניהם” — that they shall be the judges, for this expression לפני applies to the judge: “ועמדו שני האנשים אשר להם הריב לפני ה׳ לפני הכהנים והשופטים” (“then both parties to the dispute shall stand before YHWH, before the priests and the judges”) (Deuteronomy 19:17); “עד עמדו לפני העדה למשפט” (“until he stands before the assembly for judgment”) (Numbers 35:12); “לפני כל יודעי דת ודין” (“before all who know law and judgment”) (Esther 1:13). And they further expounded (ibid.) “לפניהם” — and not before laymen (הדיוטות), because it is written in the ordinances “והגישו אדניו אל האלהים” (“and his master shall bring him to the Elohim”) (Exodus 21:6), “עד האלהים יבא דבר שניהם” (“until the matter comes to the Elohim”) (Exodus 22:8), and it is also written “ונתן בפלילים” (“and he shall give through the assessors”) (Exodus 21:22) — these are the expert ordained judges, ordained through Moses our master. Therefore He said here that these ordinances shall be set before the Elohim whom He mentions, and not before gentiles, and not before one who is not a judge according to the Torah — who is a layman in this matter — for it is forbidden to come before him just as it is forbidden to come before gentiles. And even though it is known that this layman knows the line of the law and would judge him properly, he is forbidden to appoint him as a judge and to cry out to him to compel his opponent to be judged before him, and the layman himself is forbidden to judge them. And even though the Sages mentioned these two categories (gentiles and laymen) together, there is a distinction between them: if the two litigants wished to come before a layman of Israel, it is permitted, and once they have accepted him, their law is law. But before gentiles it is forbidden to come before him to be judged by their laws forever, even if their laws were like ours in that matter."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ע\"ז resolved as עבודה זרה (idolatry); הקב\"ה rendered HaKadosh Baruch Hu; פח: resolved as 88b (Gittin 88b); שמות ל טו resolved as Exodus Rabbah 30:15 per the Midrash Rabbah citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:54.143436940Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}